mplate.spec — CART-1267', function ()\n","before":"test('k8s: the POD TEMPLATE is where the API types put it — CronJob spec.jobTemplate.spec.template.spec — CART-1267', function ()\n"}},"id":"1790896960.071307-edit"},{"verb":"edit","invocation":{"verb":"edit","touched":[".claude/skills/cartograph/references/questions.md"],"args":{"file":".claude/skills/cartograph/references/questions.md","after":"| What does a Kubernetes object REFERENCE without owning it (the soft edges the garbage collector never walks: ConfigMaps / Secrets by env, envFrom and volumes, imagePullSecrets, the ServiceAccount, a PVC's claim, an Ingress backend's Service, a selector's pods) — which resolve in the release, which the cluster provides, which DANGLE, and which Service selects no pod? Where does each kind keep its pod template? | `K.attach(...)` as above, then `s.soft` = { resolved, selects, cluster, optional, implicit, dangling = { \"Deployment/web <path> names Secret/x, absent from <release>\" }, empty = { \"Service/api spec.selector{} selects no pod in <release>\" } }; resolved references are `references` edges, selector matches `selects` edges (a workload's own selector makes none); `K.summary` prints both findings. NOTHING IS LISTED: `require('cartograph.k8sapi')` READS Kubernetes' own Go API types (kinds from register.go, groups from GroupName, fields from json tags, embedding, imports resolved per package, cluster scope from `+genclient:nonNamespaced`) and DERIVES the pod-template paths (`CronJob spec.jobTemplate.spec.template.spec`) and every reference with its target kind from the source's own words (the json stem, a reference's own `kind` field first, a selector's doc comment, a suffix-only match only where the doc names the kind in full); `tools/k8sapi.lua <kubernetes checkout>` writes the shipped `lua/cartograph/k8sapi_table.lua` (84 kinds, 38 cluster-scoped, 9 pod-template kinds, 608 references at kubernetes 9f929771aca0) and `--check` exits 1 on drift. Absent = cluster-provided when the target kind is cluster-scoped (StorageClass, Node: a frontier), optional when the reference says so, implicit for the `default` ServiceAccount, else dangling. DELETION FRONTIER (CART-0139): `s.orphans` = { live = n, candidates = { \"<Kind>/<name> in <release> — no static reference …\" }, dark = { … the release ships <custom kinds> … } } over DATA kinds derived from the table (reference targets minus workloads, routers and cluster-scoped kinds): LIVE when a reference resolves, `~` CANDIDATE when none does (never auto-delete: an operator may read it by name), DARK when the release ships a kind the API table does not know (a custom resource may reference it); a kustomize `kind: Component` directory is composed with a base — its unresolved selectors / references are counted `composed`, not findings. PROBES (CART-0834): every field typed Probe (readiness / liveness / startup, on containers, init and ephemeral containers) is read at the derived paths; a gRPC probe is a CALL the kubelet makes on the Health contract — `K.link_probes(data)` (the post-pass runs it after proto mints the rpc nodes) draws `use` edges k8 = \"probes\" from the manifest to every rpc node whose wire is `/grpc.health.v1.Health/Check` (microservices-demo: 54 probes, 162 edges to 3 vendored copies); no Health rpc in the graph = a frontier; httpGet probes are counted, never minted as gRPC. CART-1267, CART-1296 |\n","before":"| What does a Kubernetes object REFERENCE without owning it (the soft edges the garbage collector never walks: ConfigMaps / Secrets by env, envFrom and volumes, imagePullSecrets, the ServiceAccount, a PVC's claim, an Ingress backend's Service, a selector's pods) — which resolve in the release, which the cluster provides, which DANGLE, and which Service selects no pod? Where does each kind keep its pod template? | `K.attach(...)` as above, then `s.soft` = { resolved, selects, cluster, optional, implicit, dangling = { \"Deployment/web <path> names Secret/x, absent from <rel